WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.403 --> 00:00:21.865
Mr. Baker had large, rounded shoulders, a massive head, and an intelligent face that sloped down to a

2
00:00:22.005 --> 00:00:23.646
pointy, grayish-brown beard.

3
00:00:24.766 --> 00:00:30.668
His red nose and cheeks, plus the slight tremor of his hand, reminded me of Holmes' suspicion that

4
00:00:30.688 --> 00:00:32.008
the man had a drinking problem.

5
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:38.043
Baker's worn black coat was buttoned high, and his collar was turned up against the cold.

6
00:00:38.903 --> 00:00:43.366
Thin wrists poked out of his coat sleeves, and there was no sign of a shirt underneath.

7
00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:49.069
His appearance gave the impression of a well-educated man who had fallen on hard times.

8
00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:55.413
"We've kept these things a few days," Holmes said, "and looked in the newspaper for an ad in

9
00:00:55.453 --> 00:00:56.634
the Lost and Found section.

10
00:00:57.194 --> 00:00:58.295
Why didn't you advertise?"

11
00:00:59.195 --> 00:01:00.296
Our visitor looked ashamed.

12
00:01:01.208 --> 00:01:03.930
"Money isn't so easy to come by these days," he said.

13
00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:08.354
"I figured the thugs who attacked me also took my hat and the bird.

14
00:01:09.275 --> 00:01:12.497
I didn't want to spend more money in a hopeless attempt to recover them."

15
00:01:13.638 --> 00:01:17.021
"Naturally. By the way, we had to eat the bird."

16
00:01:17.041 --> 00:01:18.422
"You ate it?"

17
00:01:19.043 --> 00:01:21.044
said our visitor, half rising from his chair.

18
00:01:21.064 --> 00:01:23.967
"Yes, it would have spoiled otherwise.

19
00:01:24.567 --> 00:01:26.429
But we have another goose for you.

20
00:01:27.109 --> 00:01:29.171
It's the same weight and perfectly fresh.

21
00:01:29.811 --> 00:01:30.432
Will that do?"

22
00:01:31.572 --> 00:01:33.934
"Oh certainly, certainly," Mr.

23
00:01:33.954 --> 00:01:35.415
Baker said with a sigh of relief.

24
00:01:35.435 --> 00:01:42.359
"We still have the feathers, feet, and the gullet of your original bird, if you wish━" The man

25
00:01:42.439 --> 00:01:43.760
burst into a hearty laugh.

26
00:01:45.582 --> 00:01:49.604
"They are no use to me except as souvenirs of my misadventure," he said.

27
00:01:49.624 --> 00:01:51.766
"No, sir, but I thank you.

28
00:01:52.466 --> 00:01:54.387
I'll just take this new bird as a replacement."

29
00:01:55.128 --> 00:01:58.330
Sherlock Holmes glanced sharply at me and gave a slight shrug.

30
00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.977
"Here's your goose and your hat," Holmes said.

31
00:02:01.997 --> 00:02:05.379
"By the way, where did you get the other bird?

32
00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:08.962
I like good poultry, and I haven't seen a better goose."

33
00:02:09.783 --> 00:02:12.926
Baker stood and tucked his newly gained property under his arm.

34
00:02:12.946 --> 00:02:15.988
"My friends and I frequent the Alpha Inn.

35
00:02:16.589 --> 00:02:19.651
This year Windigate, the owner, started a goose club.

36
00:02:20.452 --> 00:02:24.335
We pay a little bit every week, and at Christmas we each get a bird.

37
00:02:25.376 --> 00:02:27.718
I was all paid up, but you know the rest.

38
00:02:28.639 --> 00:02:29.820
I'm indebted to you, sir.

39
00:02:30.724 --> 00:02:33.226
This knitted cap doesn't fit my age or style."

40
00:02:34.086 --> 00:02:38.949
He switched hats and, with comical pomposity, bowed solemnly and strode off.

41
00:02:38.969 --> 00:02:41.110
"So much for Mr.

42
00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:43.932
Henry Baker," Holmes said after the door closed.

43
00:02:43.952 --> 00:02:47.594
"It's obvious he knows nothing about the Blue Carbuncle.

44
00:02:48.154 --> 00:02:49.255
Are you hungry, Watson?"

45
00:02:50.055 --> 00:02:50.916
"Not particularly."

46
00:02:50.936 --> 00:02:54.297
"Let's skip dinner and follow this clue while it's still hot."

47
00:02:54.317 --> 00:02:56.359
"By all means," I agreed.

48
00:02:57.239 --> 00:03:01.093
It was bitterly cold, so we put on woolen scarves and heavy coats.

49
00:03:02.054 --> 00:03:08.918
Outside, the stars shone in a cloudless sky, while the breath of the passersby blew out like gun

50
00:03:08.978 --> 00:03:16.162
smoke. Our footsteps echoed crisp and loud as we swung through Wimpole Street, Harley Street, and onto Oxford

51
00:03:16.202 --> 00:03:21.805
Street. Fifteen minutes later we were at the Alpha Inn, a small corner bar.

52
00:03:22.725 --> 00:03:27.508
Holmes pushed open the door and ordered two beers from the ruddy-faced, aproned bartender.

53
00:03:28.724 --> 00:03:32.905
"Your beer will be excellent if it's as good as your geese," Holmes said.

54
00:03:32.925 --> 00:03:34.306
"My geese!"

55
00:03:35.046 --> 00:03:36.206
The man seemed surprised.

56
00:03:37.146 --> 00:03:39.887
"Yes. I just met a member of your goose club."

57
00:03:41.167 --> 00:03:41.888
"Oh, I see.

58
00:03:42.548 --> 00:03:45.089
But they aren't our geese," the bartender said.

59
00:03:45.109 --> 00:03:46.729
"Whose are they then?"

60
00:03:46.749 --> 00:03:49.670
"We buy them from a salesman in Covent Garden."

61
00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:51.690
"I know some of them.

62
00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:52.891
Which one was it?"

63
00:03:53.411 --> 00:03:54.511
Holmes asked casually.

64
00:03:54.531 --> 00:03:56.712
"Breckinridge is his name."

65
00:03:58.336 --> 00:04:01.017
"Hmm, I don't know him," said Holmes, tipping the bartender.

66
00:04:01.037 --> 00:04:04.339
"Well, here's to your good fortune, sir.

67
00:04:04.799 --> 00:04:05.159
Good night."

68
00:04:06.119 --> 00:04:07.140
"Let's find Mr.

69
00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:11.302
Breckinridge," said Holmes as we left the bar and went out into the frosty air.

70
00:04:11.322 --> 00:04:17.604
"Remember, Watson, there's a goose at one end of this mystery, but at the other end is a

71
00:04:17.624 --> 00:04:21.846
man who will spend seven years in jail unless we prove his innocence."

72
00:04:22.926 --> 00:04:24.807
"We may discover he's guilty," I said.

73
00:04:26.476 --> 00:04:28.538
"Yes, but we know something the police don't.

74
00:04:29.178 --> 00:04:30.600
It may be his only chance.

75
00:04:31.501 --> 00:04:33.382
Let's follow this lead to the end!"

76
00:04:34.323 --> 00:04:40.248
We crossed Holborn Street and strode through a zig-zag of slums to Covent Garden Farmer's Market.

77
00:04:41.369 --> 00:04:47.775
The largest stall said "Breckinridge," and inside, a man with a horselike face was helping a boy close

78
00:04:47.815 --> 00:04:48.356
up for the night.

79
00:04:48.376 --> 00:04:50.378
"Good evening, sir.

80
00:04:51.138 --> 00:04:51.919
Cold, isn't it?"

81
00:04:52.379 --> 00:04:52.800
said Holmes.

82
00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:57.361
The salesman nodded and shot a questioning glance at my companion.

83
00:04:58.822 --> 00:05:00.143
"Sold out of geese, I see."

84
00:05:00.863 --> 00:05:03.104
Holmes pointed to the bare slabs of marble.

85
00:05:03.124 --> 00:05:07.105
"There'll be more tomorrow morning," said Breckinridge.

86
00:05:07.125 --> 00:05:09.947
"That's no good," Holmes said.

87
00:05:09.967 --> 00:05:12.488
"Well, try the next stall."

88
00:05:13.188 --> 00:05:13.848
The man pointed.

89
00:05:13.868 --> 00:05:19.491
"But you were highly recommended by the bartender at the Alpha Inn," Holmes said.

90
00:05:19.511 --> 00:05:22.452
"Oh yes, I sent him a couple of dozen."

91
00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:24.260
"Fine birds.

92
00:05:25.101 --> 00:05:25.921
Where did you get them?"

93
00:05:26.341 --> 00:05:26.902
Holmes asked.

94
00:05:28.062 --> 00:05:32.064
To my surprise the question provoked a burst of anger from the salesman.

95
00:05:32.084 --> 00:05:34.125
"All right, mister."

96
00:05:34.885 --> 00:05:36.446
He glared and folded his arms.

97
00:05:37.126 --> 00:05:38.507
"What do you want?"

98
00:05:38.527 --> 00:05:43.929
"Simply I'd like to know who sold you the geese you gave to the Alpha."

99
00:05:43.949 --> 00:05:45.610
"I won't tell you!"

100
00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:48.111
"Oh well, it's not important.

101
00:05:48.611 --> 00:05:50.232
But why are you so angry?"

102
00:05:50.944 --> 00:05:54.446
"You'd be angry too if you'd been pestered about it as much as I have.

103
00:05:55.287 --> 00:05:58.369
Everyone has been coming and asking me, ‘Where are those geese?'

104
00:05:58.409 --> 00:06:00.050
and ‘Who did you sell them to?'

105
00:06:00.751 --> 00:06:04.133
You'd think they were the only geese in the world for all the fuss."

106
00:06:04.153 --> 00:06:08.416
"Well, I have no connection to those people," Holmes said.

107
00:06:08.436 --> 00:06:12.679
"But if you won't tell me, the bet is off, that's all.

108
00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:17.122
I wagered good money that the bird I ate was country bred."

109
00:06:17.142 --> 00:06:19.103
"Then you've lost your money.

110
00:06:19.523 --> 00:06:21.816
It was town bred," snapped Breckinridge.

111
00:06:23.097 --> 00:06:27.079
"I know poultry, and it was nothing of the kind," Holmes scoffed.

112
00:06:28.259 --> 00:06:30.540
"I've been selling birds since I was a boy.

113
00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:34.241
I tell you, all those birds that went to the Alpha were town bred."

114
00:06:34.261 --> 00:06:37.023
"You'll never persuade me to believe that."

115
00:06:37.043 --> 00:06:38.483
"Will you bet then?"

116
00:06:38.503 --> 00:06:41.865
"I'd just be taking your money because I know I'm right."

117
00:06:42.685 --> 00:06:44.005
Holmes reached into his pocket.

118
00:06:44.025 --> 00:06:46.306
"But I'll bet this coin."

119
00:06:47.167 --> 00:06:48.507
The salesman chuckled grimly.

120
00:06:50.412 --> 00:06:51.833
"Bring me the books, Bill."

121
00:06:52.693 --> 00:06:57.875
The boy brought over a thin notebook and a fat accounting ledger, and opened them under a hanging

122
00:06:57.915 --> 00:06:59.916
lamp. "Now then, Mr.

123
00:06:59.956 --> 00:07:01.637
Know-it-all," the salesman said.

124
00:07:01.657 --> 00:07:03.418
"See this little book?

125
00:07:03.998 --> 00:07:05.439
It lists my suppliers.

126
00:07:06.039 --> 00:07:07.339
See that third name?

127
00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:08.520
Read it."

128
00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:14.402
"‘Mrs. Oakshott, 117 Brixton Road,'" read Holmes.

129
00:07:14.422 --> 00:07:17.484
"A town address obviously.

130
00:07:18.036 --> 00:07:20.778
Now find the next-to-last page in that ledger."

131
00:07:21.699 --> 00:07:22.339
Holmes found it.

132
00:07:22.359 --> 00:07:24.101
"Now what's the last entry?"

133
00:07:24.121 --> 00:07:29.345
"‘December 22, twenty-four geese purchased from Mrs.

134
00:07:29.465 --> 00:07:30.726
Oakshott,'" Holmes read.

135
00:07:30.746 --> 00:07:32.527
"And underneath?"

136
00:07:33.207 --> 00:07:34.048
the salesman prompted.

137
00:07:34.068 --> 00:07:35.869
"‘Sold to Mr.

138
00:07:35.909 --> 00:07:37.250
Windigate of the Alpha.'"

139
00:07:38.351 --> 00:07:40.293
"What do you say now?"

140
00:07:40.813 --> 00:07:41.514
said Breckinridge.

141
00:07:42.454 --> 00:07:44.496
Sherlock Holmes looked deeply embarrassed.

142
00:07:45.068 --> 00:07:50.533
He threw his coin down and turned away like a man whose disgust is too deep for words.

143
00:07:51.754 --> 00:07:54.676
A few yards off, he stopped and laughed silently.

144
00:07:54.696 --> 00:08:01.702
"Ah, Watson, the horse-racing sheet sticking out of his pocket told me he likes to gamble.

145
00:08:02.582 --> 00:08:07.927
We couldn't have paid him to tell us, but we got all our information by making a bet.

146
00:08:08.727 --> 00:08:10.929
Well, Watson, the end is near.

147
00:08:11.810 --> 00:08:12.690
Do we see this Mrs.

148
00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:14.232
Oakshott tonight or tomorrow?

149
00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:18.026
It's clear someone else━" He stopped talking suddenly.

150
00:08:18.567 --> 00:08:21.613
A loud commotion had broken out in the stall we'd just left.
